        329.86480712890625,
        277.6725769042969,
        372.4514465332031,
        291.6717834472656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Soft (0.7)",
      "bbox": [
        213.38499450683594,
        288.13836669921875,
        244.31234741210938,
        295.3517761230469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "0.720 ± 0.107",
      "bbox": [
        266.5416564941406,
        287.355712890625,
        313.1626281738281,
        301.35491943359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "17.7 ± 0.707",
      "bbox": [
        329.8648376464844,
        287.355712890625,
        372.45147705078125,
        301.35491943359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Soft (0.9)",
      "bbox": [
        213.38499450683594,
        297.82073974609375,
        244.31234741210938,
        305.0341491699219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "0.704 ± 0.114",
      "bbox": [
        266.5416564941406,
        297.0380859375,
        313.1626281738281,
        311.03729248046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "17.3 ± 0.872",
      "bbox": [
        329.8648376464844,
        297.0380859375,
        372.45147705078125,
        311.03729248046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the velocity. While the system is initialised with physical coordinates Z0, both networks are unconstrained and unregularised",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        331.86895751953125,
        541.4402465820312,
        341.6298828125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "for all t > 0. As discussed above, this means that the system is free to learn a latent manifold sufficient for image",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        343.823974609375,
        541.43994140625,
        352.8899230957031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "reconstruction, even if the latent representation is a non-linear transformation of the true physical coordinate system. We",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        355.9383544921875,
        541.4358520507812,
        364.84490966796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "employ a SIREN (Sitzmann et al., 2020) architecture to model the latent dynamics. The network takes the concatenated input",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        367.89337158203125,
        541.4400024414062,
        376.7999267578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "\u0002h(Zj)",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        378.135498046875,
        84.70545196533203,
        389.35089111328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ζj",
      "bbox": [
        94.66805267333984,
        379.5899658203125,
        102.3236083984375,
        389.35089111328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "\u0003",
      "bbox": [
        103.2229995727539,
        378.135498046875,
        107.37740325927734,
        384.5115661621094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "∈R5 and outputs the state derivative. The update rule in the first setting is Zj+1 = Zj+Net(",
      "bbox": [
        110.14099884033203,
        376.2333679199219,
        470.76959228515625,
        396.1670837402344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "\u0002",
      "bbox": [
        470.77398681640625,
        378.1344909667969,
        474.92840576171875,
        384.51055908203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "h(Zj)",
      "bbox": [
        474.92498779296875,
        379.5899658203125,
        500.0384521484375,
        389.35089111328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ζj",
      "bbox": [
        510.00103759765625,
        379.5899658203125,
        517.6575927734375,
        389.35089111328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "\u0003",
      "bbox": [
        518.5570068359375,
        378.1344909667969,
        522.71142578125,
        384.51055908203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": ") ∆j.",
      "bbox": [
        522.7080078125,
        379.68896484375,
        543.183837890625,
        389.4508972167969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In the second setting, where we invoke resampling at nearly all filtering steps, we make an additional additive noise",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        391.8033752441406,
        541.4406127929688,
        400.7099304199219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "assumption, omit concatenating the input and let Zj+1 = Zj + Net(h(Zj)) ∆j + ζj. Without this assumption, the neural",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        403.5999755859375,
        541.4403686523438,
        413.94073486328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "network is free to learn to neglect the noise, and in the first setting, we indeed found that the learnt dynamics network often",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        415.71435546875,
        541.4380493164062,
        424.62091064453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "had this issue. This can happen in end-to-end learning since minimising the optimisation objective does not necessarily",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        427.66937255859375,
        541.7863159179688,
        436.575927734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "require the network to optimise for a good filtering distribution, and reducing the randomness might, for instance, help",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        439.6243591308594,
        541.4408569335938,
        448.5309143066406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "stabilise optimisation.",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        451.5793762207031,
        142.901611328125,
        460.4859313964844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In both settings, the network consists of 3 hidden layers of 256 units with sine activations (ω0 = 8.0 in the first layer). The",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        469.35296630859375,
        541.4392700195312,
        479.6937255859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "decoder maps h(Zj) to observations and is identical in both settings. It first processes the input via two linear layers (16",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        481.3079528808594,
        541.4367065429688,
        491.06988525390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "and 256 units), reshaping the output into a spatial feature map of size 4 × 4 × 16. This is followed by three transposed",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        492.45599365234375,
        541.4448852539062,
        509.7410888671875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "convolution layers (kernel size 3 × 3, stride 2) with output channels of 32, 16, and 1, respectively, to upsample to the final",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        504.4119873046875,
        541.44384765625,
        521.6970825195312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "32 × 32 image. ReLU activations are used for all hidden layers, while the output layer is linear.",
      "bbox": [
        55.191001892089844,
        516.3670043945312,
        435.4733581542969,
        533.652099609375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "L. Bayesian neural network training",
      "bbox": [
        55.44000244140625,
        544.4039306640625,
        240.85321044921875,
        555.1516723632812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We apply SMC for a larger-scale problem: training a (partial) Bayesian neural network (Zhao et al., 2024; Sharma et al.,",
      "bbox": [
        54.97200012207031,
        565.2713623046875,
        542.6896362304688,
        574.1779174804688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "2023, pBNN). There are two steps moving from training a classical BNN to training a pBNN with SMC sampler. First,",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        577.2263793945312,
        542.6805419921875,
        586.1329345703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "pBNNs only have priors on a (small) subset of the neural network parameters. This results in a latent-variable model to learn",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        589.181396484375,
        541.4400024414062,
        598.0879516601562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "from the data, where computing the posterior distribution is easier than that of the full BNN. Let us denote the pBNN by",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        601.1363525390625,
        541.7890014648438,
        610.0429077148438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "x 7→fθ,z(x), where θ and z stand for deterministic and stochastic parameters, respectively. Second, the prior construction",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        612.125,
        541.4395751953125,
        629.4100952148438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "is no longer static or explicit (e.g., z being a Gaussian). Instead, z is assumed to follow a Markovian dynamics motivated",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        624.887939453125,
        541.4412231445312,
        633.9539184570312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "by Zhao et al. (2024); Chang et al. (2022); Freitas et al. (2000) so that data are modelled as independent conditionally on a",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        637.0023803710938,
        541.4361572265625,
        645.908935546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "latent process. Specifically, we apply the pBNN setting and construct an SSM",
      "bbox": [
        55.439998626708984,
        648.9573974609375,
        365.58563232421875,
        657.8639526367188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "p(zj | zj−1) = N(zj; ρ zj−1, 1 −ρ2),",
      "bbox": [
        223.19100952148438,
        669.1461791992188,
        373.6930236816406,
        689.1580810546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "pθ(Dj | zj) = Softmax(fθ,zj; Dj),",
      "bbox": [
        225.26597595214844,
        685.322021484375,
        368.0460510253906,
        704.1021118164062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(38)",
      "bbox": [
        525.510009765625,
        678.79638671875,
        542.1077880859375,
        687.7029418945312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "for CIFAR10 classification, where Dj is a batch data at surrogate time j (training step), and we choose ρ = 0.99.",
      "bbox": [
        55.44000244140625,
        708.14697265625,
        506.08013916015625,
        718.7069702148438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "26",
      "bbox": [
        293.4590148925781,
        733.21240234375,
        303.4216003417969,
        742.1189575195312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    }
